A||N||13||Eriamjh||16 Things I learned doing my Front suspension...||||1||
Z||000000||Eriamjh||12-19-2001||06:56 PM||||Here's what I learned rebuilding the front suspension on my '87 Coupe:<P>1.)  Buy all your parts first before you start.  I spent 3.5 weeks waiting for the coil springs to come in.  Only 2 weeks wasted there!<P>2.)  Spray everything down with penetrant days before starting to remove bolts.  Spray them every day.  Buy stock in WD-40.<P>3.)  Torch the steering rack bolts before you even loosen them with a wrench.  Let the heat loosen them.<P>4.) Assume you will have to drill and tap the steering rack and sway-bar bolts.  6 of 8 broke on mine.  Cheap GM bolts are made of putty!<P>5.)  Be sure to notify the alignment shop before bringing it in.  They get ticked when a car comes in and the suspension is way out.<P>6.)  Either buy or rent all the tools before you start.  Check all the bolts you will remove with your tools to see if you need anything you don't already have.  I didn't have a shallow 18MM socket or wrench for the suspension bolts and had to borrow them from work.  I just have to remember to return them.<P>7.) Rent or buy a grinder for the suspension bolts.  They WILL be siezed to the inner sleeves of the bushings.  You will not be able to remove the nut and slide them out.<P>8.)  Buy all new hardware for the suspension.  GM still sells the upper control arm bolts for far less then the Fiero Store.  If you get them from a bolt shop, get 250mm X 12MM bolts, grade 10.9.<P>9.)  Buy extra grease for the poly bushings.  You will run out with what comes with the bushing kit from the Fiero Store.  I used Reese trailer hitch grease from Wal-Mart based on a recommendation from this forum.<P>10.)  Be sure to turn the zerk fittings of the lower ball-joints inboard.  You can't get a grease gun on them if they face outboard to the wheel.  Make sure you have angled zerks.<P>11.)  Be sure to remove the bolt that holds the rack to the steering column completely before trying to remove the rack.  The bolt holds the rack by a ridge of metal if it's loose for safety.<P>12.)  Be sure to tighten to steering coupler bolt to the rack completely.  My first try and there was still play between the column and the rack.  Bad.  Have a helper move the column while you watch the rack's input shaft.  They will move together when that bolt is tight enough.<P>13.)  You may want to have a collision shop look at the frame before starting.  Mine apparently is bent a bit and the shop couldn't achieve the proper camber on one side.  I still have to have the car straightened.<P>14.) Do your suspension work in warm weather like spring or fall.  Winter is too damn cold!!<P>15.)  Remove the splash shields before starting.  Makes for more room.  Buy new fasteners (the plastic ones) from a place that sells the Au-Ve-Co parts in bulk like a fastener/bolt shop.  I paid $7 for 25 pieces instead of $2 for 2 pieces at a local auto parts place.  Buy in advance (see #1).<P>16.)  Post frequently to this forum for any questions you might have.  The people who read this forum are the best in the world and I couldn't have finished this job without their help (thanks, Nashco!).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||12-19-2001||07:22 PM||||Allot of good notes there.... plan the same way if you do the struts.<P>One trivia note....<P>Common Item Of Confusion. Even did it in my strut article... (I'll remember to fix it someday...)<P>Metric bolts are "classed" not "graded" like SAE. A metric class 10.9 bolt is the equivalent of a Grade 8 SAE bolt.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||10
Z||000002||Fierobruiser||12-19-2001||08:05 PM||||If anyone needs just about any fastener and some other asssorted pieces for their Fiero, try this site. <A HREF="http://www.ardeldistributors.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ardeldistributors.com/</A> <P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||12-20-2001||01:45 AM||||Nice to see some "by the way" information, that's always the stuff that comes back to bite you in the ass.  Glad I could help.<P>Just a note/correction, you said the bolts should be 250 mm long...????  I think my 88 used 90 mm long bolts.  I can't imagine GM would use a 250 mm long bolt for ANYTHING! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Bryce<BR> <IMG SRC="http://home1.gte.net/jbnash/nashcosig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||12-20-2001||02:03 AM||||Nothing that long? Dude, check your 88 REAR suspension lower lateral/toe links  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>(PS: I think he has an 87)<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B><BR>Just a note/correction, you said the bolts should be 250 mm long...????  I think my 88 used 90 mm long bolts.  I can't imagine GM would use a 250 mm long bolt for ANYTHING!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000005||Banzai||12-20-2001||06:54 AM||||I think you've covered about everything. WD-40 isn't the best penetrant out there. Go with PB Blaster or another high grade penetrant. Don't forget to soak the tie-rod ends. Use anti-sieze on alot of the bolts during reassembly.<BR>Good Post<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tigger||12-20-2001||07:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>Just a note/correction, you said the bolts should be 250 mm long...????  I think my 88 used 90 mm long bolts.  I can't imagine GM would use a 250 mm long bolt for ANYTHING!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think he's referring to the UCA bolt.  Pre 88's use one long bolt across both bushings.<P>Very Good post!<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000007||Eriamjh||12-22-2001||10:13 AM||||Yes, I have an '87.  While some of the notes might apply to an '88, not all.<P>||||reg||1
